DERIVED={
1:('1','The introduction explicitly separates a rigid-link joint chain from a mobile differential-drive system controlled by wheel velocities.'),
2:('1','Link-attached frames move as the mechanism articulates. Kinematics describes that geometry, not the forces or torques causing motion.'),
3:('1','Joint configurations are inputs and Cartesian tool poses are outputs, so this is forward rather than inverse kinematics.'),
4:('1','The stated FK role maps joint-configuration commands into Cartesian observations; it does not optimize the policy or replace a torque model.'),
5:('1,2,3','The source lists collision geometry, differentiable trajectory optimization and link geometry inside simulators. It calls FK relevant to sim-to-real but does not guarantee successful transfer with no mismatch.'),
6:('1','Configuration DOF counts independent joint coordinates, not matrix entries or merely three position coordinates.'),
7:('1','An unconstrained spatial rigid-body pose has three translational plus three rotational freedoms; the planar 2+1 alternative describes a different task space.'),
8:('1','A revolute joint permits rotation about its fixed axis; a prismatic joint permits translation along its fixed axis. Each has one independent coordinate here.'),
9:('1','The slide numbers the immobile base 0, the first moving link 1 and the free-end link n.'),
10:('1','A joint connects links while restricting allowable relative motion. It neither releases all constraints nor fixes every joint against all motion.'),
11:('1','The definition is direct surface contact between the two bodies; point-only contact is not the lower-pair definition supplied.'),
12:('1','The visible labels give cylindrical P+R two freedoms, planar P-P-R three, and spherical R-R-R three.'),
13:('1','For the explicitly assumed fixed-lead screw, axial displacement is determined by rotation; the two visible kinds of motion are coupled, unlike the independent cylindrical P+R coordinates.'),
14:('1','The planar block can translate twice and rotate once; the ball-and-socket has three rotations. These are the pictured three-freedom mechanisms.'),
15:('1','The four named parameters are link length a, twist alpha, offset d and joint angle theta.'),
16:('1','DH systematically assigns frames and derives serial-link transforms. It is not a claim that inverse kinematics is unique or all poses reachable.'),
17:('1','A rigid link fixes the relationship between its supported axes through their common-normal distance a and twist alpha.'),
18:('1','The straight a_(i−1) line has perpendicular markers at both joint axes. It is not the curved body length or base-to-tool reach.'),
19:('1','The displayed connection description names d and theta; a and alpha describe the link’s supported-axis geometry instead.')}
